---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatCssUnits from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatCssUnits.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="em-to-px">
  <DevFormatCssUnits client:load from="em" to="px" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The formula depends on the parent</h2>
    <pre><code>px = em × parent font size

Parent 16px:  1.25em = 20px
Parent 14px:  1.25em = 17.5px
Parent 20px:  1.25em = 25px</code></pre>
    <p>
      This is the practical difference from rem: the same em value means different things in different
      places, so &ldquo;what is 1.25em?&rdquo; has no answer without context. Set the parent field above to
      the real computed size to get a useful number.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding the parent font size</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open devtools and select the element.</li>
      <li>Switch to the <strong>Computed</strong> panel, not Styles, which shows the declared value.</li>
      <li>Read <code>font-size</code>. This is a resolved pixel value after all inheritance.</li>
      <li>For a <code>font-size</code> declared in em, select the parent element and read its computed size.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The two-bases problem</h2>
    <p>
      One rule can use two different bases at the same time, and almost everyone gets this wrong once.
    </p>
    <pre><code>/* Parent computes to 16px */
.badge &#123;
  font-size: 0.75em;   /* 0.75 × 16 = 12px   (parent's size) */
  padding: 0.5em;      /* 0.5  × 12 = 6px    (this element's size) */
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The padding is 6px, not 8px, because <code>font-size</code> resolves first, and the other properties
      of the element resolve against the result. If the spacing of a component is smaller than you
      expected, this is almost always the cause.
    </p>

    <h2>Common em values at a 16px parent</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>em</th><th>px</th><th>Where you see it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0.5</td><td>8</td><td>Gap between an icon and its label</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.625</td><td>10</td><td>Vertical padding on a compact button</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.75</td><td>12</td><td>Badge and caption text</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.875</td><td>14</td><td>Secondary text</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>16</td><td>Same as the surrounding text: icons, inline spacing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.25</td><td>20</td><td>Sub-heading, wide horizontal padding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.5</td><td>24</td><td>Heading, or a large spacing step</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>32</td><td>Section heading</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>em inside a media query</h2>
    <p>
      A special case: inside <code>@media</code>, em is always relative to the initial font size of the
      browser (the default or the size the user chose), never to a font size you declared. So
      <code>@media (min-width: 48em)</code> applies at 768px for a default user and at 960px for a user
      whose default is 20px, which is usually the behaviour you want. It is also not affected by a project
      that sets <code>html &#123; font-size: 62.5% &#125;</code>, so em breakpoints are more robust than rem
      breakpoints in such projects.
    </p>

    <h2>Auditing an existing component</h2>
    <p>
      Converting the em values of a component to px checks that it is internally consistent. If the
      padding, gap and radius all fall on the same spacing grid at the base size, the component scales
      cleanly. If one of them is 6.5px, someone adjusted it by eye, and it will look wrong at a different
      size.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
